format session-log-v1
stamp 20260821.160424
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title The mark law seated, and the resolver built before the first fold
prompt seat the mark law and build the resolver first
think Took the ordering literally. The mark law is the Lindy half -- it governs every future mark -- and the resolver is the crux, the gate the first fold must not cross without. Neither the fold nor any repointing was opened this round.
think Learned Rishi's real shape before writing in it, since a witness written against a remembered language is a witness that reds on the first run. Found three bounds the tree's README does not spell out: there is no ordering operator at all, only == and !=; slice refuses an out-of-range end rather than clamping; and a let inside for-each does not escape the loop, so there is no accumulator.
think Found a fourth by colliding with it -- `matches` is a Rishi infix operator (`<string> matches <pattern>`), so `let matches = where ...` bound a name the parser already owned and every later use read UndefinedName. Renamed to by_basename. A fifth quiet vocabulary collision, found by writing rather than by reading for it.
think Wrote the census before the resolver, because a tool built to repair a number should be built against the number rather than against a memory of it. Good thing: the first census contradicted the survey.
think Caught my own measurement red in the survey. The 454-of-583 figure resolved every reference as root-relative, yet a Markdown link inside crux/ writing `archive/20260618-...md` is correct relative to its own file and my reading called it broken. Rebuilt the walker to resolve relative-to-citer first and root-relative second -- the way a reader actually follows a link -- and the honest tree-wide figure came out 4,138 broken of 19,787. The session-log slice re-measured at 455 of 584 -- the survey's 454 of 583 held, the one added being this round's own log, so its does-not-claim hedge resolves rather than corrects.
think Caught a second measurement red on the first run of the scan proper: it reported 33,441 references where the same grep by hand reported 25,009. The extra 8,425 live in `seed/`, the gitignored public-seed projection of this same tree, which my interactive grep excludes and a bare sh does not. A census that counts the projection counts the field twice, so seed, vendor, and .git are now excluded by name with the reason written beside them.
think Chose the resolver's five readings so the two that cost nothing come first, the pure computation before the index, and the index before giving up. Reading one borrows the shell's own path resolution by testing from the citing file's directory rather than writing a normalizer -- a normalizer here would be one more thing that could be silently wrong about `../`.
think Made the fold rule the third reading rather than the fourth on purpose. It is computed from the basename alone and needs no index, so it keeps working for rooms not yet folded, and proving it that way is the whole argument for repeating the day inside the filename.
think Proved that claim by construction rather than by assertion. The witness plants a room the resolver has never seen, places one file at date/YYYYMMDD/, resolves the FLAT reference, and gets the folded home back with no index consulted -- then sweeps the room before asserting, so cleanup happens whether the proof passes or fails.
think Set the broken-reference ceiling with no slack rather than with headroom. The caravan carry meter carries headroom because carry legitimately fluctuates; broken references only ever go down, so slack there would only hide the next fold's damage.
think Named the census's second bound out loud after it bit. The resolver's own documentation and its witness cite deliberately stale paths -- that is what a resolver demonstration IS -- and counting them made the ceiling rise every time the proof got stronger. Excluded dated_path_* by glob, with the reason written in the scan: those are fixtures, not defects.
think Wrote the scan's control corpus as a throwaway git tree with a known answer -- one reference that lands and one that does not -- because a walker whose whole job is to report the tree's own breakage cannot be checked against the tree.
obs The ceiling proved itself twice during the round before anything shipped. It refused the witness's own stale examples, and then refused two illustrative paths in the mark law itself, each built from a real-looking stamp and a slug naming no file. Rewrote them with letter placeholders, since a rule about references should not carry a broken one to make its point. It fired a third time on the research note that proposed the law, so the habit became a line of the law rather than a third exclusion in the scan -- illustrate with placeholders, cite only what exists. And the counter-example could not be shown either, since writing the wrong shape is writing it.
obs Kept the rule's amendment to waymark-ladders surgical. A waymark is a NAME drawn for a ladder and it keeps its place; only the numbered rung after it retires. Step four now says so in both the Claude and Cursor twins, and every already-numbered ladder keeps every number it wrote.
obs Recorded the refined numbers as a dated addendum to the survey rather than editing its body. The survey is hours old and its numbers were honest for the method it used; accrete-never-break asks that a better method accrete beside it.
loom census=dated_path refs_total=19787 refs_home=15649 refs_broken=4138 recoverable=3939 ambiguous=16 gone=183 ceiling=4138
loom census=session_logs_slice unique_paths=584 broken=455 resolve=129
loom census=bounds seed_refs_excluded=8425 wall_s=2.7
loom witness=dated_path_witness verdicts=5 red_paths=3 control=known_answer exit=0
file .claude/rules/stamp-and-name.md the mark law -- stamp and name, never an ascending mark; count never number; the whole stamp stays in every filename
file .cursor/rules/stamp-and-name.mdc the Cursor twin of the same law
file .claude/rules/waymark-ladders.md step four amended -- a waymark is a name and keeps its place; the numbered rung retires
file .cursor/rules/waymark-ladders.mdc the same amendment in the Cursor twin
file context/LEXICON.md three seated rows -- stamp and name, date fold, dated-path resolver
file crux/REMEMBER.md the mark law added to the inner loop, so the next lap applies it without a restart
file tools/dated_path_resolve.rish the resolver -- five readings, first hit winning, inventing nothing
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_scan.sh the census walker, its two bounds named, its ratchet with no slack
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_control.sh a throwaway corpus whose right answer is known before the walker runs
file tools/dated_path_witness.rish the proof -- five verdicts, the fold rule on a room that never folded, three RED paths, the census under its ceiling
file external-research/20260821-152409_the-standfast-read-marks-rooms-and-the-vocabulary-pruning.md dated addendum recording what the census refined
recommend check-in (checkpoint) the gate is open and the fold is not -- arc one's law and resolver stand GREEN, so the next door is folding session-logs (1,492 flat, the one room actually over the cap) to date/YYYYMMDD/ and seating the 256-file bound as its own witness, which is a word rather than a kg since it moves files
